  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWith a west-northwest flow and 850mb temperatures only around 4C\nover the warm Great Lakes, scattered lake effect rain showers\nhave been present this afternoon, mainly along and north of the\nNY Thruway corridor. This pattern is expected to continue\ntonight right through Friday, with mainly dry conditions\nelsewhere. Otherwise, it will be breezy both this afternoon and\nFriday afternoon with occasional gusts of 20-30 mph possible.\nTemperatures will be seasonable with lows tonight in the upper\n30s to mid 40s and highs on Friday in the mid 50s to upper 60s.\n\nOne more rain-free day is expected on Saturday with high\npressure in control. Mostly sunny skies are expected with highs\nin the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe center of the remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias will\nmove into the Ohio Valley on Sunday as the circulation begins to\ndissipate into an open wave. This tropical moisture will surge\nnortheastward into our region on Sunday, especially Sunday\nafternoon through Sunday night. Moderate to locally heavy\nrainfall will be possible during this timeframe, but with the\nquick movement of this moisture, combined with dry antecedent\nconditions, the threat for flooding looks low at this time.\n\nThis moisture will depart the area on Monday, which will bring a\nreturn to mainly dry conditions. That being said, a few\nlingering light rain showers cannot be ruled out, especially in\nthe morning. A frontal system may bring the next chance for rain\nshowers by Tuesday, but timing and placement of this front is\nuncertain this far out.",
